WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.500
Our ocean.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.800
Nearly 4 billion years old

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.500
Self-regulating,

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.500
self-healing

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.500
the source of all life on earth.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.500
Yet, it is in danger

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.200
Threatened by a truly global problem:

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.000
Marine pollution

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.000
From the remotest Pacific islands

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.500
To the bottom of the Mariana Trench

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.000
To right here on the beaches of Malta

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Oil spills,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.000
ship emissions

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.500
industrial waste and pesticides

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.500
– all poison vast stretches of water.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000
10 million tonnes of litter are dumped in the sea every year.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.500
400 kilos per second.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.500
By 2050 our ocean could contain more plastic than fish.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Pollution is destroying marine life.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.800
Plastics alone are killing one million seabirds and 100,000 marine mammals a year.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Microplastics threaten to unhinge whole ecosystems.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.500
These problems don’t stop at sea.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.200
Contaminants such as mercury accumulate in the food chain

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.500
Affecting the health of millions of people.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:50.000
With 80% of marine litter coming from land sources, everyone is responsible.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.000
But the solution is at hand.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.000
And it offers a great opportunity.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.800
Marine pollution costs billions.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.000
Switching to circular economies

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.500
could make up for this loss and more

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.000
with environmental, economic, and social gains for all.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.500
So to reverse the trend of ever-increasing marine pollution 

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.000
How can we minimise waste?

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.000
How can we prevent plastics from reaching the sea?

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.000
How can we stop marine pollution?

